118 In a MIL-53 coordination environment, $\mathrm{Fe(m)}$ should be relatively easy to reduce. If so, then rapid luminescence quenching by electron transfer from photo-excited BDC to $\mathrm{Fe(m)}$ would account qualitatively for the observations. If the photo-prepared molecular exciton can migrate from linker to linker (for example, via F¨orster energy transfer) only a comparatively small fraction of the $\mathbf{Al}(\mathrm{{_{III}})}$ ions would need to be displaced by $\mathrm{Fe(m)}$ in order to engender fluorescence quenching. Although no evidence for or against such amplified quenching is presented in the original report, it’s occurrence might account for the observed low $\mathrm{Fe(m)}$ detection threshold.   
